    <item>
      <title>AMD P-State Driver For Linux 7.4 Will Tune For Zen 6 Client CPUs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-AMD-P-State-Zen-6</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-AMD-P-State-Zen-6</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 19:03:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Linux 7.4 looks like it will be a very exciting kernel update for AMD customers. On the AMD Radeon graphics side &lt;a href="https://www.p2horonix.com/news/Linux-7.4-AMDGPU-HDMI-2.1-Go"&gt;HDMI 2.1 is being enabled by default&lt;/a&gt; with the AMDGPU driver with the HDMI FreeSync, VRR, and ALLM features in tow. On the AMD Ryzen client CPU side, there will be Zen 6 tuning with the AMD P-State CPU frequency scaling driver.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Being submitted ahead of the Linux 7.4 merge window are &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Zen-6-Client-CPPC-Changes"&gt;the AMD P-State Energy Performance Preferences (EPP) adjustments for Zen 6 client CPUs&lt;/a&gt;. The ACPI EPP will behave differently on Zen 6 client CPUs and it includes different tunings for the performance and dense/efficiency cores along with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/x86-Core-Types-Linux-7.3"&gt;the new AMD low-power core type&lt;/a&gt;.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Beyond different values for all three Zen 6 core types, the AMD P-State driver is seeing adjustments where the boost ratio is calculated from frequency values rather than an arbitrary performance scale.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=zen6_tune_74" alt="AMD Zen 6 tuning EPP for Linux 7.4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The AMD Zen 6 client EPP tuning patches were sent out along with some other AMD P-State fixes via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/linux-pm/03c0a0d4-20d2-475b-ab7c-5b1a3742cf92@amd.com/"&gt;this pull request&lt;/a&gt; to the Linux power management subsystem's "-next" branch. The pull is explicitly marked for Linux 7.4.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The Linux 7.4 merge window will open in the second half of October while thee stable kernel release may be out by the end of December depending upon how the remainder of the v7.3 and v7.4 cycles play out. With next-gen AMD Ryzen CPUs rumored for launch around CES in January or otherwise in H1'2027, it looks like Linux 7.4 will be out right on time and a wonderful Christmas gift for AMD Linux customers.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>SDL3 Ported To HarmonyOS / OpenHarmony</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/SDL3-Ported--To-HarmonyOS</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/SDL3-Ported--To-HarmonyOS</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 16:54:08 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX GAMING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxgaming.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The SDL3 library commonly used for games and applications for cross-platform hardware and software abstractions now has upstream support for Huawei's HarmonyOS and OpenHarmony. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Famous Linux game porter Ryan "Icculus" Gordon was sponsored by the Outfit7 game studio to bring the SDL3 library to HarmonyOS/OpenHarmony. HarmonyOS is the proprietary, distributed operating system developed by Huawei for use on smartphones, tablets, PCs, smart devices, and more. HarmonyOS features a micro-kernel design. OpenHarmony is the open-source distributed OS that in simple terms is an offshoot of HarmonyOS. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=sdl3_harmonyos_med" alt="SDL3 HarmonyOS" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This SDL3 port to HarmonyOS/OpenHarmony is already  in use by some commercial games, according to Icculus. It's been tested on real hardware and as of today is merged to upstream SDL Git. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL/pull/16281"&gt;This pull&lt;/a&gt; goes over all the details on SDL3 for HarmonyOS.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ubuntu 26.10 Set To Deliver Better Performance For Intel Core 3 Wildcat Lake</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 15:34:07 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p&gt;While Ubuntu 26.04 LTS is working fine out-of-the-box on new Intel Core 3 &amp;quot;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Wildcat+Lake"&gt;Wildcat Lake&lt;/a&gt;&amp;quot; laptops like the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/chuwi-unibook-wildcat-lake"&gt;CHUWI UniBook&lt;/a&gt;, next month's release of Ubuntu 26.10 will help deliver better performance out of these low-cost laptop options.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake&amp;image=ubuntu_2610_wcl_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake&amp;image=ubuntu_2610_wcl_1_med" alt="Ubuntu 26.10 on CHUWI UniBook" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The upcoming release of Ubuntu 26.10 with the Linux 7.2 kernel upgrade over Linux 7.0 on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS plus the newer Mesa 26.2 graphics driver stack and other software upgrades will allow for greater performance on new Intel Wildcat Lake hardware. Intel Panther Lake stands to similarly benefit as well but today's article is looking squarely at the low-end with the Core 3 Wildcat Lake performance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake&amp;image=ubuntu_2610_wcl_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=ubuntu-2610-wildcat-lake&amp;image=ubuntu_2610_wcl_2_med" alt="Ubuntu 26.10 Wildcat Lake" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Ubuntu 26.04 with all stable release updates was compared to the performance of Ubuntu 26.10 in its current development state as of 13 September. This comes ahead of the Ubuntu 26.10 beta due toward the end of September and then the official Ubuntu 26.10 release due out on 15 October.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="https://phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/result.svgz" alt="Ubuntu 26.04 LTS vs. Ubuntu 26.10 Intel Wildcat Lake" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Ubuntu 26.04 LTS and Ubuntu 26.10 daily were compared on the CHUWI UniBook with Intel Core 3 304 SoC, 8GB of RAM, and 256GB NVMe SSD. It's a straight-forward comparison for measuring the performance impact of the Ubuntu  26.10 upgrade with its new software packages. A follow-up article will also be exploring the Wildcat Lake impact from the optional Ubuntu 26.10 amd64v3 binaries.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-rand-read-io_uring-no-4kb-5-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Random Read, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: No, Block Size: 4KB, Job Count: 5, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-rand-read-io_uring-yes-4kb-1-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Random Read, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: Yes, Block Size: 4KB, Job Count: 1, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-rand-write-io_uring-no-4kb-1-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Random Write, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: No, Block Size: 4KB, Job Count: 1, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-seq-read-io_uring-no-2mb-5.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Sequential Read, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: No, Block Size: 2MB, Job Count: 5, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-seq-read-io_uring-yes-2mb-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Sequential Read, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: Yes, Block Size: 2MB, Job Count: 1, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-seq-write-io_uring-no-2mb-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Sequential Write, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: No, Block Size: 2MB, Job Count: 1, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/flexible-io-tester-seq-write-io_uring-yes-2mb-1.svgz" alt="Flexible IO Tester benchmark with settings of Type: Sequential Write, Engine: IO_uring, Direct: Yes, Block Size: 2MB, Job Count: 1, Disk Target: Default Test Directory. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With recent versions of the Linux kernel at large we have been seeing some nice I/O improvements. This was the case here even with the budget Wildcat Lake laptop from CHUWI seeing improved I/O performance on Ubuntu 26.10 thanks to its Linux 7.2 kernel. Linux 7.3 also has more in store for I/O performance albiet out of scope for Ubuntu 26.10.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/ddracenetwork-1920-x-1200-fullscreen-vulkan-default-multeasymap.svgz" alt="DDraceNetwork benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Mode: Fullscreen, Renderer: Vulkan, Zoom: Default, Demo: Multeasymap. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/ddracenetwork-1920-x-1200-fullscreen-vulkan-default-multeasymap-total-frame-time.svgz" alt="DDraceNetwork benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Mode: Fullscreen, Renderer: Vulkan, Zoom: Default, Demo: Multeasymap, Total Frame Time. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Not only was the storage performance better with Ubuntu 26.10, but the Wildcat Lake integrated graphics have a nice boost on Ubuntu 26.10 thanks to Linux 7.2 and Mesa 26.2 graphics drivers.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/tesseract-1920-x-1200-2.svgz" alt="Tesseract benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For some OpenGL and Vulkan workloads the low-end integrated graphics with Wildcat Lake were seeing some significant improvements out of the Ubuntu 26.10 upgrade.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/xonotic-1920-x-1200-low-2.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Effects Quality: Low. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/xonotic-1920-x-1200-high-2.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Effects Quality: High. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/xonotic-1920-x-1200-ultra.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Effects Quality: Ultra. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/xonotic-1920-x-1200-ultimate.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Effects Quality: Ultimate. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/yquake2-vulkan-on-on-1920-x-1080.svgz" alt="yquake2 benchmark with settings of Renderer: Vulkan, AF: On, MSAA: On, Resolution: 1920 x 1080. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/yquake2-vulkan-off-on-1920-x-1080.svgz" alt="yquake2 benchmark with settings of Renderer: Vulkan, AF: Off, MSAA: On, Resolution: 1920 x 1080. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/yquake2-vulkan-on-off-1920-x-1080.svgz" alt="yquake2 benchmark with settings of Renderer: Vulkan, AF: On, MSAA: Off, Resolution: 1920 x 1080. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/yquake2-vulkan-off-off-1920-x-1080.svgz" alt="yquake2 benchmark with settings of Renderer: Vulkan, AF: Off, MSAA: Off, Resolution: 1920 x 1080. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/vkmark-1920-x-1200-mailbox.svgz" alt="VKMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 1920 x 1200, Present Mode: Mailbox. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The graphics improvements alone make Ubuntu 26.10 a worthwhile upgrade for Wildcat Lake laptop owners. If wanting to stick to an Ubuntu LTS release, you can always use a PPA or similar for obtaining the newer Mesa OpenGL and Vulkan drivers.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-3-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 3, Compression Speed. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-8-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 8, Compression Speed. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-8-ds.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 8, Decompression Speed. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-19-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Compression Speed. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-19-ds.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Decompression Speed. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/zstd-compression-3-long-mode-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 3, Long Mode, Compression Speed. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;In the CPU focused workloads, the performance varied from flat to some modest uplift.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-aes-256.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: AES-256. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-aes-256-decrypt.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: AES-256, Decrypt. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-serpent-xts.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: Serpent/XTS. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-serpent-xts-decrypt.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: Serpent/XTS, Decrypt. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-chacha20poly1305.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: ChaCha20Poly1305. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/botan-chacha20poly1305-decrypt.svgz" alt="Botan benchmark with settings of Test: ChaCha20Poly1305, Decrypt. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With the Botan library shipped in the Ubuntu archive, there is now Botan 3.12 with Ubuntu 26.10 and showing some nice improvements with certain algorithms.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/gnu-radio-fir-filter.svgz" alt="GNU Radio benchmark with settings of Test: FIR Filter. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/gnu-radio-fdf.svgz" alt="GNU Radio benchmark with settings of Test: FM Deemphasis Filter. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/gnu-radio-hilbert-transform.svgz" alt="GNU Radio benchmark with settings of Test: Hilbert Transform. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For other Ubuntu packages like GNU Radio there were performance gains in some areas but also some losses.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/7-zip-compression-compression-rating.svgz" alt="7-Zip Compression benchmark with settings of Test: Compression Rating. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The 7-Zip build provided by Ubuntu Linux was faster with its 26.10 state.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/numpy-benchmark.svgz" alt="Numpy Benchmark benchmark with settings of . Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Numpy shipped by Ubuntu 26.10 was also faster than the 26.10 release.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/perl-benchmarks-interpreter.svgz" alt="Perl Benchmarks benchmark with settings of Test: Interpreter. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Even a slightly faster Perl too.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/openssl-sha256.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA256. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/openssl-sha512.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA512. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/openssl-chacha20-poly1305.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: ChaCha20-Poly1305. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With Ubuntu 26.10 is now OpenSSL 4.0 being used rather than OpenSSL 3.5 on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS. For SHA512 performance there was better performance but also some losses for other tests.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/cryptsetup-pbkdf2-sha512.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of PBKDF2-sha512. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Cryptsetup's PBKDF2-sha512 performance saw a huge leap in performance on Ubuntu 26.10 while the other Cryptsetup benchmarks were unchanged.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pybench-tfatt.svgz" alt="PyBench benchmark with settings of Total For Average Test Times. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-chaos.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: chaos. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-float.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: float. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-sphinx.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: sphinx. Ubuntu 26.04 LTS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-regex_dna.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: regex_dna. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-gc_collect.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: gc_collect. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/pyperformance-json_loads.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: json_loads. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With both Ubuntu 26.04 and 26.10 using  the Python 3.14 series, there wasn't much to see out of the Python scripting benchmarks in this round of testing.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/ubuntu-2604-lts-vs-ubuntu-2610-intel-wildcat-lake/geometric-mean-of-all-test-results-result-composite-u20lvu21iwl.svgz" alt="Geometric Mean Of All Test Results benchmark with settings of Result Composite, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS vs. Ubuntu 26.10 Intel Wildcat Lake. Ubuntu 26.10 13 Sep was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When running 160+ benchmarks on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS and Ubuntu 26.10 on this CHUWI UnniBook $449 laptop powered by the Intel Core 3 Wildcat Lake, the upcoming Ubuntu release was around 4% faster overall. The most exciting performance improvements on this low-end laptop were found around I/O and the graphics performance. That 4% improvement on Ubuntu 26.10 is still well short of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/wildcat-lake-windows-linux"&gt;CachyOS being around 17% faster than Ubuntu 26.04&lt;/a&gt; on the same CHUWI UniBook Wildcat Lake laptop.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Fedora 45 Beta Released With KMSCON Console, GNOME 51 &amp; Other Updates</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Fedora-45-Beta</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Fedora-45-Beta</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:30:08 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="FEDORA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/fedora.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The beta release of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Fedora+45"&gt;Fedora 45&lt;/a&gt; is out today and right on schedule for this wonderful Linux distribution. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Fedora 45 is another feature-heavy update with using KMSCON now to replace the in-kernel console, Stratis Storage support within the Anaconda installer, restricting ptrace by default for better security, enforcing signature checking by default, and expecting package builds to be reproducible. Fedora 45 also introduces the DRM Panic front-end for a more convenient and friendly experience should you encounter any kernel problems. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_1_med" alt="Fedora Workstation 45 beta" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There are also many package updates with Fedora 45 like the GNOME 51 desktop for Fedora Workstation 45, Go 1.27, Python 3.15, the GCC 16.2 compiler, Glibc 2.44, LLVM 23, RPM 6.1, and  many other updates. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_2_med" alt="Fedora 45 beta" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Fedora 45 is  currently working toward a release plan for a 20 October debut with a contingency fallback date of 27 October. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=fedora_45beta_3_med" alt="Fedora Workstation 45 beta with GNOME 51 desktop" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on today's Fedora 45 Beta release via the &lt;a href="https://lists.fedoraproject.org/archives/list/announce@lists.fedoraproject.org/thread/FTPOQ3H47Y2MCHFVJ5C7P5CO25GDTJV3/"&gt;release announcement&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Java 27 Reaches GA With The G1 Garbage Collector By Default Everywhere</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/OpenJDK-27-Java-27</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/OpenJDK-27-Java-27</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:39:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="ORACLE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/oracle.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Oracle christened Java 27 today with the OpenJDK 27 release reaching general availability (GA) status. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With Java 27, the Garbage-First "G1" garbage collector is now enabled by default everywhere. Since JDK 9 there was the G1 garbage collector  used by default in Java server environments while now it's proven robust enough to use as the default everywhere. Compared to the Serial collector, G1 should be delivering competitive throughput, latency, memory footprint, and start-up time across all heap sizes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/523"&gt;JEP 523&lt;/a&gt; for changing to G1 by default  everywhere reads: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Since then, we have improved the G1 collector across all metrics, and testing shows that G1 is now competitive with Serial at all heap sizes. With our recent work to reduce synchronization (JEP 522), G1's maximum throughput is close to that of Serial. G1's maximum latencies have always been better than those of Serial, since G1 reclaims memory in the old generation via incremental garbage collections rather than full collections. Finally, in recent releases we have reduced G1's native memory usage to levels comparable to that of Serial. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;G1's performance is now sufficient to replace Serial in all situations in which the JVM would previously have selected Serial. It is time to stop selecting Serial by default in constrained environments. This will also make it easier to understand and reason about the JVM’s behavior."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;OpenJDK / Java 27 also adds support for Post-Quantum Hybrid Key Exchange for TLS 1.3, the newest preview of lazy constants, the seventh preview of structured concurrency support for Java, compact object headers by default, and the latest incubator on Java's Vector API. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The compact object headers by default will reduce object headers from 96 bits down to 64 bits for improving deployment density, enhancing data locality, and reducing the heap size. Since JDK 24, the compact object headers have said to proven their stability and performance to make it the new default. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=java_cup_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=java_cup_med" alt="old Java coffee cup" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the Java 27 GA release via this morning's  &lt;a href="https://mail.openjdk.org/archives/list/announce@openjdk.org/thread/ORGGLMN75HFEWP7YL3ZLGHLYHVIBJDYT/"&gt;release announcement&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sound Open Firmware 2.15 Released With AMD ACP 7.x Support, Intel UAOL</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Sound-Open-Firmware-2.15</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Sound-Open-Firmware-2.15</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:04:40 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="MULTIMEDIA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/multimedia.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Sound Open Firmware as the open-source, vendor-independent audio DSP firmware stack and driver framework is out today with a new feature update. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sound Open Firmware 2.15 introduces user-space, memory-protected module execution support.. Plus related enhancements around MMU protections and overall security so that misbehaving or buggy modules can no longer corrupt kernel memory or other moules' memory. SOF developers are working toward an end goal of being able to run untrusted vendor DSP modules. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sound Open Firmware 2.15 brings new hardware support including AMD ACP 7.x and NXP i.MX8MP Cortex-M7. AMD ACP 7.x will  presumably be with upcoming AMD Zen 6 based desktop/laptop designs. The AMD ACP 7.x audio co-processor is described as offering "SoundWire DMA/DAI, TDM DAI type, 500µs low-latency timer tuned for ACP." This goes along with other &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-ACP-7.x-Linux-7.2"&gt;recent Linux kernel driver activity for AMD ACP 7.x IP&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With this new release there is also Intel USB Audio Offload Link "UAOL" support for enabling USB audio offload from the host CPU while the DSP handles USB audio directly. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=sof" alt="Sound Open Firmware logo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For improving testing of the SOF code, there is also now a QEMU Xtensa target ass well as a POSIX and libFuzzer fuzzing platform for security hardening use. Sound Open Firmware 2.15 also incorporates a number of security fixes and additional code hardening. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sound Open Firmware 2.15 also introduces new audio processing modules of Phase Vocoder, STFT Process, IPC4 Tone generator, FFT, and  Selector modules. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the many Sound Open Firmware 2.15 changes can be found via the project's &lt;a href="https://github.com/thesofproject/sof/releases/tag/v2.15"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Firefox 157 Beta Released With Latest Enhancements</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Firefox-157-Beta</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Firefox-157-Beta</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:31:03 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="MOZILLA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/mozilla.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Firefox release train keeps on rolling. With &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Firefox-156"&gt;Firefox 156 released&lt;/a&gt;, Firefox 157 is now in beta ahead of the planned release coming up at the end of September. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Firefox 157 beta better handles HDR videos  encoded with 8-bit color formats to properly display, improved audio/video synchronization when changing the playback rate for an HTML media element, and fixing the vertical tabs sidebar in some conditions. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_1_med" alt="Firefox 157 Beta" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;On the developer side is now support for the at-rule() function for @supports and support for the "chain" value for the CSS overscroll-behavior property. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_2_med" alt="Firefox 157 Beta  Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Firefox 157 is also bringing Mozilla's latest work on their Nova UI design, including the bringing back of Firefox's compact mode option for a denser UI. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=firefox_157beta_3_med" alt="Firefox 157 Beta compact  mode" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Firefox 157 Beta can be downloaded for those interested via &lt;a href="https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/firefox/releases/157.0b1/"&gt;ftp.mozilla.org&lt;/a&gt;. Firefox 157 stable is expected on 29 September.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>New Linux Patches Improving Hibernation Speed In 2026</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Improv-Hibernation-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Improv-Hibernation-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:30:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A new patch series being worked on is enhancing the Linux kernel's hibernation allocator to allow for faster performance when hibernating the system. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux developer Youngjun Park at LG posted a request for comments (RFC) today with a set of ten patches to improve the image slot allocation and I/O around the Linux hibernation code. Youngjun Park explained in the patch series on the Linux kernel mailing list: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"This series improves how hibernation allocates swap slots for its image.  It starts from a few observations about what happens while the image is written.  With them the allocator can hand the image contiguous runs, the image I/O can be batched per run, and hibernation gets faster. Contiguous I/O pattern is friendly to flash device also."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The improvements make not only the hibernation allocator code faster but also simpler and enabling batching of image reads and writes. Testing within a QEMU VM of this tentative code found the write times reduced by 18~25% while the read times were 11~20% faster. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=framework-laptop-13-pro&amp;image=framework_13pro_1_med" alt="Linux laptop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The patches aren't yet complete but so far are looking promising for those interested in faster hibernation on Linux. Those interested can  see these initial patches out for comment on the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/linux-pm/20260915031658.1505680-1-youngjun.park@lge.com/"&gt;Linux kernel mailing list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Raspberry Pi Desktop Overhauled On Raspberry Pi OS, Adds Icon Dock</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Raspberry-Pi-Desktop-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Raspberry-Pi-Desktop-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:14:58 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="RASPBERRY PI" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/raspberrypi.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Raspberry Pi Desktop on Raspberry Pi OS has seen a major  overhaul to better modernize the desktop computing experience on the ARM64 single board computers. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Raspberry Pi Desktop hasn't evolved too much over the years but with the latest OS image and package updates out today come a big UI/UX modernization effort with significant changes. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=raspberry_pi_desktop_1" alt="Raspberry Pi Desktop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Raspberry Pi Desktop now features an icon dock by default, to complement or replace the existing taskbar.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=raspberry_pi_desktop_2" alt="Raspberry Pi Desktop launcher screen" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There is also now a graphical application launcher screen as a replacement to the main menu widget on Raspberry Pi OS. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=raspberry_pi_desktop_3" alt="Raspberry Pi Desktop configuration area" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The improved desktop also has a new task list, new customization options for the dock and desktop, an improved screenshot taking experience, and other enhancements. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More screenshots and details on the overhauled Raspberry Pi Desktop can be found via today's announcement on &lt;a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/news/an-updated-look-for-the-raspberry-pi-desktop/"&gt;RaspberryPi.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Patch Coming To Fix Under-Reported AMD Zen 5 TLB Sizes</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Fix-AMD-Zen-5-TLB-Sizes</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Fix-AMD-Zen-5-TLB-Sizes</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:05:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A minor reporting issue on Linux that has gone unaddressed until now is for properly conveying the Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) sizes on AMD Zen 5 processors. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With Zen 5 CPUs, AMD began reporting the TLB sizes as multiples of 32. But the Linux kernel was never adapted to check the necessary bit and make the appropriate calculation. There is now a patch to correct that so the AMD EPYC and Ryzen Zen 5 TLB sizes will be reported correctly under the likes of &lt;em&gt;/proc/cpuinfo&lt;/em&gt;. This also carries forward for Zen 6 and future AMD CPUs using this bit for reporting TLB sizes as multiples of 32. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=amd_zen5_tlb_sizes" alt="AMD Zen 5 TLB size reporting fix" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/tip/tip.git/commit/?id=228200f695c0f92c5fd06ff0bdcf94bb0d2ca7f5"&gt;This patch&lt;/a&gt; fixes the AMD TLB size reporting. Currently it's in the x86/cpu branch. With it being in that branch rather than one of TIP's "urgent" branches, it will presumably be submitted for the Linux 7.4 merge window rather than the urgent route as a Linux 7.3 fix.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
